{
  "dom_id": "step-q116337",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The intensity of radiation $I,$ in milliroentgen/hour ($\\text{mR}/\\text{h}$), spreading out from an X-ray device is inversely proportional to the **square** of the distance $d,$ measured in meters, between the device and the object being radiated. If a device that measures the radiation intensity registers $82\\,\\text{mR}/\\text{h}$ at a distance of $5\\,\\text{m}.$ What equation gives the relationship between $I$ and $d?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since the intensity $I$ is inversely proportional to the square of the distance $d,$ we must have\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $82\\,\\text{mR}/\\text{h}$ when $d=5\\,\\text{m}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}82 & =\\frac{k}{{5}^{2}} \\\\ 82 & =\\frac{k}{25} \\\\ 82\\cdot 25 & =k \\\\ 2\\,050 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{2\\,050}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
